Capacity Planning Kya Hai?
Simple shabdon mein:

Tumhara system kitna load handle kar sakta hai, aur usse zyada aane par kya hoga?

Teen cheezein decide karni hain:

Throughput — kitne requests per second (QPS/RPS)

Latency — kitni der mein reply milega (ms)

Saturation point — kahan se system breakdown shuru hota hai

Real example:
Ek WhatsApp group mein 200 log hain. Subah 8 baje sab "Good Morning" bhejte hain.
Agar tera server sirf 100 requests/sec handle karta hai, toh 200 requests aayengi toh 100 log fail ho jayenge — yeh capacity planning failure hai.

🔢 Step 1: Traffic Estimation — Ground Up
1.1 Daily Active Users se Shuru
hinglish
DAU = 1 million (maan lo)
Har user average:
  - 10 reads
  - 1 write
  - 1 search
Total actions/user/day = 12

Total actions/day = 1M × 12 = 12M
1.2 QPS (Queries Per Second) Mein Convert
hinglish
Seconds in a day = 24 × 3600 = 86,400
Average QPS = 12,000,000 / 86,400 ≈ 139 QPS
Important: Ye average hai. Real mein traffic uniform nahi hota.

1.3 Peak QPS — The Real Killer
hinglish
Peak factor = 5x (common for social apps)
Peak QPS = 139 × 5 ≈ 700 QPS

Morning 8-10 AM, evening 7-9 PM peak hota hai.
1.4 Burst Traffic — Viral Moment
hinglish
Agar koi post viral ho jaye (Jaise CR7 post):
  - Read QPS suddenly 700 se → 50,000 tak ja sakta hai
  - Isliye auto-scaling + caching zaroori hai
💾 Step 2: Storage Estimation — Data Ka Size Samjho
2.1 Har Object Ka Size
hinglish
Example: Twitter tweet
  - Tweet ID: 8 bytes
  - User ID: 8 bytes
  - Text: 280 chars = 280 bytes
  - Timestamp: 8 bytes
  - Metadata (likes, retweets count): 50 bytes
  - Overhead (database ka internal): ~100 bytes

Total per tweet ≈ 450 bytes ≈ 0.45 KB
2.2 Indexes — Jo Log Bhool Jaate Hain
hinglish
Database indexes:
  - Primary key index: tweet_id
  - Secondary indexes: user_id, timestamp, hashtags
  
Har index ≈ 30-50% of data size hota hai
So indexes = 0.45 KB × 0.5 = 0.225 KB extra

Effective size per tweet = 0.45 + 0.225 = 0.675 KB
2.3 Replication aur Overhead
hinglish
Production mein hota hai:
  - Primary + 2 replicas = 3x storage
  - WAL (Write Ahead Log) = 0.2x
  - Temp space for queries = 0.1x

Total multiplier = 3 + 0.2 + 0.1 = 3.3x

Final per tweet storage = 0.675 KB × 3.3 ≈ 2.2 KB
2.4 Real Example: 1 Saal Ki Tweets
hinglish
1 billion tweets/year (India ka approx)
Storage = 1B × 2.2 KB = 2.2 TB raw
With replication (3 copies) = 6.6 TB
With indexes = ~9 TB
With backups = ~18 TB (backup 2x)
🧠 Step 3: Memory Estimation — Cache Ka Game
3.1 Working Set Concept
hinglish
80/20 rule:
  - 20% data gets 80% traffic
  - Yeh 20% data = "hot data"

Maan lo total data = 100 TB
Hot data = 20 TB
3.2 Cache Mein Rakhenge Kitna?
hinglish
Typical caching strategy:
  - Last 7 days hot data
  - Per day: 1 TB new hot data
  - 7 days = 7 TB

Cache memory needed = 7 TB
But 7 TB RAM impossible hai ek server mein.
Toh distributed caching (Redis Cluster) lagana padega.

3.3 Memory per Request
hinglish
Har request ke liye temporary memory:
  - Request object: 1 KB
  - Response object: 10 KB
  - Query execution: 50 KB
  - Connection buffer: 10 KB

Total per request ≈ 71 KB

Peak QPS = 10,000 pe:
  - Memory for in-flight requests = 10,000 × 71 KB ≈ 710 MB
  - Yeh sirf active requests ke liye
🌐 Step 4: Network Bandwidth Estimation
4.1 Bandwidth Formula
hinglish
Bandwidth needed = (Average request size + response size) × Peak QPS

Example:
  - Request: 1 KB
  - Response: 50 KB
  - Peak QPS: 10,000

Bandwidth = (1 + 50) × 10,000 = 510,000 KB/sec = 510 MB/sec
4.2 Network Card Capacity
hinglish
- 1 Gbps card = 125 MB/s theoretical
- Real throughput = ~100 MB/s
- Isliye 510 MB/s ke liye ~6 network cards chahiye (bonding)

Ya better: 10 Gbps card lagao
4.3 Intra-DC vs External Traffic
hinglish
Internal (same data center):
  - Web server → Cache server
  - Cache miss → DB server
  - Latency: 0.5 ms

External (user → server):
  - India to Mumbai server: 10 ms
  - India to US server: 200 ms
  - Isliye region-based deployment zaroori hai
🧮 Step 5: CPU aur Core Estimation
5.1 Har Core Kitna Handle Karega?
hinglish
Typical numbers (real benchmarking se):
  - Simple JSON API: 500-1000 QPS/core
  - With DB query: 50-100 QPS/core
  - With image processing: 5-10 QPS/core
  - With ML inference: 1-2 QPS/core

Example: Video encoding
  - Har video chahiye 10 seconds CPU time
  - 100 videos/minute = 1000 seconds CPU per minute
  - Isliye parallel processing with GPU
5.2 Core Count Calculation
hinglish
Target Peak QPS = 10,000
Per core capacity (API + DB) = 200 QPS

Cores needed = 10,000 / 200 = 50 cores

With overhead (context switch, GC, etc) = +30%
Total cores = 65 cores

Servers (16 cores each) = 65/16 ≈ 5 servers
⚠️ Step 6: Bottlenecks — Real Problems
6.1 Database Connection Pool
hinglish
Har DB connection = ~2 MB memory + 1 core thread
Max connections = cores × 2

16 core server → max 32 DB connections
Agar QPS zyada hai toh connection queue lagega
6.2 Disk IOPS — Hidden Bottleneck
hinglish
HDD: 100-200 random IOPS
SSD: 50,000-100,000 IOPS
NVMe: 500,000+ IOPS

Example: 
  - 10,000 write QPS
  - HDD impossible (needs 10,000 IOPS)
  - SSD min 20,000 IOPS chahiye
6.3 Lock Contention — Multithreading Ka Dushman
hinglish
Agar 50 threads ek hi lock pe wait karein:
  - Effective throughput = single thread
  - Java synchronized, Python GIL example

Solution: 
  - Shard karo (hash based)
  - Lock-free data structures
  - Read-write locks
📊 Step 7: Real Production Formula (Mera Personal Experience Se)
hinglish
Effective Capacity = (Hardware Limit × 0.7) - (Bottleneck Penalty)

Jahaan:
  - 0.7 = safe margin (30% headroom for spikes)
  - Bottleneck Penalty = slowest component ka effect

Example:
  - CPU limit = 10,000 QPS
  - DB limit = 5,000 QPS
  - Network limit = 20,000 QPS
  - Bottleneck = DB (5,000)

Effective = 5,000 × 0.7 = 3,500 QPS safe
🎯 Step 8: Interview Mein Kaise Impress Karein?
Ye Batana Ki Tum Depth Jaante Ho:
Not just QPS — but P95, P99 latency

Not just storage — but WAL, indexes, compaction

Not just servers — but rack, DC, region redundancy

Not just average — but traffic pattern (poisson vs uniform)

Example Answer (Hinglish Mein):
"Sir, maine maan liya DAU 10M hai.
Average QPS ~10k aayega, peak ~50k.
Har request 50KB ki hai, toh bandwidth ~2.5 GBps chahiye.
Database mein indexing + replication se effective storage 3x ho jayega.
Sabse bada bottleneck hoga DB connections, isliye read-heavy hai toh Redis cache lagana padega with 80% hit rate.
Aur cache ke liye working set ~5TB hai, isliye distributed Redis cluster with 30 nodes.
Sab server 60% utilization pe rakhenge taaki spike handle ho sake."

🔥 Last Tip: Yaad Rakhna
Cheez	Formula
Peak QPS	Avg QPS × (2 to 10)
Storage with replication	Raw × (3 to 5)
Cache memory	Working set × 1.5
Servers needed	(Cores required / cores per server) × 1.3
Response time budget	Network + Queue + Processing

